# Commit messages

When creating a git commit, write the message so it states **exactly the changes that were made** (what files/areas and what was done), not a vague summary or only the why.

## Format

- Subject line: concrete list or short inventory of the changes.
- Optional body: bullets naming the concrete edits if the subject cannot fit them all.
- Prefer precise verbs: add, update, fix, remove, rename — tied to the actual change.

## Examples

```text
✅ Add live/as-of filters to signal book, allocation, dashboard, and HRP optimizer; badge scoring paths in Signal Book; add Risk seed/proxy labels; empty-state pipeline CTAs; PRD and contract tests.

❌ Close v1 honesty gaps.
❌ Improve research desk integrity.
```

Do not substitute a high-level “why” message for a description of the actual diff.
